# Generated catalog files, normalised to LF in the repository.
#
# WHY. Something on the office machine rewrites products.json with Windows
# line endings. The content is byte-identical, but git sees every one of its
# 386,310 lines as changed — so the file sits permanently "modified", and the
# nightly SanMar sync lands on top of it as a 2,537-line merge conflict across
# catalog data. That happened on 16 Oct 2026 and had to be unpicked by hand
# before an unrelated change could be pushed.
#
# `text eol=lf` makes git normalise these to LF on the way in and check them
# out as LF. A tool that rewrites them with CRLF then produces no diff at all,
# which is the honest answer: nothing about the file actually changed.
products.json  text eol=lf
crops.json     text eol=lf
reviews.json   text eol=lf

# Same problem, same fix, for the pages themselves. On 21 Oct alive-product.html
# showed as fully modified with not one byte of real change — something on this
# machine had rewritten it with CRLF. Every tracked .html is stored LF, so this
# normalises without churn.
*.html         text eol=lf
